<!--Post: 9284--><!--Time: 1109462038-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST9284"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Jeff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Jeff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, February 26, 2005 - 3:53 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Obviously .. he never went away.. and although I think the Wichita Police &amp; everyone thought he went away.. there are now murders surrounding Wichita that are thought to have been done by BTK.  Even now.. it&#39;s up to 1991.. and others suspected in the mid-90&#39;s, additional ones like to day in 1984, Weigerle was in 1j986/1987.  So, he continued in the 80&#39;s and possibly thru the mid 90&#39;s.  His newer victims were just outside the city of Wichita, and since they didn&#39;t share info with other agencies, it is likely that is the reason for others to not be able to connect BTK originally to them.  Now, the FBI investigating cold cases are making links.  I think they&#39;ll find more as they go.. and that BTK never did &#34;quit&#34; or disappear, just changed location and mode of operation &#40;dumping bodies in fields like the 2 new ones identified were&#41;.   <BR> <BR>So.. due to the criticism on the Wichita Police failing to share info could have resulted in him functioning longer and not catching him sooner. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 9284-->
<!--Post: 9348--><!--Time: 1109481613-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST9348"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, February 26, 2005 - 9:20 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Jeff I agree that the primary barricade to capturing BTK was lack of cooperation between departments and agencies. But he&#39;s caught. It should have been sooner but they got him. <BR> <BR>LE not sharing information and thus not capturing criminals is as old as criminology. It will always happen, sad to say. But DNA and other forensic advances will mean fewer and fewer unsolved crimes, especially serial crimes. The FBI was in on this case years and years ago; I wouldn&#39;t say the delay was only WPD&#39;s doing. It&#39;s a good time. He&#39;s caught.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 9348-->
<!--Post: 9359--><!--Time: 1109483528-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST9359"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, February 26, 2005 - 9:52 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->George - do you think that, because of DNA being the ultimate solution in all these modern cases, they might as well just dump the letters and other stuff to the public to a much greater degree along the way?  I am betting it would precipitate many more instances of friends and family members turning in suspects.  It seems that the &#34;validating&#34; value of suspect writings and whatnot is minimal compared to DNA, but their utility in prompting the public seems beyond question.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 9359-->
<!--Post: 9360--><!--Time: 1109483605-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST9360"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, February 26, 2005 - 9:53 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Serial murderers do not simply stop murdering. It seems highly likely the BTK Strangler claimed more victims during the 1980s, 1990s and early 2000s. Serial murderers only stop murdering when they are incarcerated or dead. Serial murderers derive sexual gratification from murdering and unless his sexual desire declined during the last 25 years, he remained active pursuing victims. The important question is: How many more victims did Dennis Rader claim?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 9360-->
<!--Post: 9410--><!--Time: 1109502145-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST9410"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Yomama<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Yomama<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, February 27, 2005 - 3:02 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->i&#39;m almost positive that he never stopped...!!!they never do.  hopefuly they will start delving into any unsolved murders and start sharing information,he may of traveled at times, family vacations hunting trips, fishing trips etc. le needs to share all murders and suspicous deaths state to state at all times especialy with travel being so easy and fast these days ! i hope law enforcement does everything they can now that they got dennis to make sure that they find every murder he comitted. better late then never and i hope law inforcement learns from this.  i thank God they got him!!! FINALY!!!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 9410-->
<!--Post: 9521--><!--Time: 1109544206-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST9521"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, February 27, 2005 - 2:43 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Pax, if the authroities are certain they have the perps DNA, I don&#39;t see the harm in releasing a large amount of information to the public, while saving a few specific details, of course. That&#39;s where I agree with John Douglas the most vehemently: become procative!  Of course, most serial murderers don&#39;t write letters and play word games but there will always be information to share. <BR> <BR>I am squeamish about composites. The Lake Sammamish Ted Bundy composite is most famous for being very accurate and other composites have also been accurate and therefore been of help. But composites can also be inaccurate, and flashing an inaccurate composite on the local news a few times leaves an image which most people probably won&#39;t be able shake off. One case in point EAR/ONS. During the EAR &#34;phase&#34; a composite was issued and it turned out to be a composite of an innocent man. The intentions were good but it was a disaster. It is true that soon after the composite was in the paper, EAR changed locations. Whether this was the result of a composite &#40;however inaccurate&#41; or the result of the composite driving home in a general sense to EAR the seriousness of his rapes isn&#39;t known.  <BR>Night Driver: As you know this case well, please correct any mistakes about EAR I may have made. <BR> <BR>That said, I&#39;d still go with composites. But glimpses of strangers put down on paper and then broadcast will always be a difficult decision -- first impressions and all that. But even here we have more hope. Cameras are everywhere and they become more accurate and more inexpensive to run as time passes. Yes, I favor data dumps, but not right away -- unless police have absolutely nothing to go on.  <BR> <BR>Final thing. If I read or viewed a police department spokesperson saying they fear a serial killer is at large and they have little to go on, I wouldn&#39;t consider such a statement &#34;weakness&#34; or incompetence, I would applaud it!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 9521-->
<!--Post: 9530--><!--Time: 1109545423-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST9530"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Nightdriver<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Nightdriver<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, February 27, 2005 - 3:03 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->George, how about the drawing of the Hispanic guy? That really matches Dennis The Menace, doesn&#39;t it. Yes, that first composite of EAR turned me off in relation to two guys I liked at the time. I think it&#39;s more correct to say that it was the drawing on the left in re Maggiore that was either him or someone whose face resembled him way too close, that made him move the attacks and then leave town. It is still very possible that the one drawing is really EAR, and I have a possible name to associate with the other drawing, just don&#39;t have enough background yet.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 9530-->
<!--Post: 9827--><!--Time: 1109655938-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST9827"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, February 28, 2005 - 9:45 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The guy in the wool cap? I don&#39;t remember if that was Kevin Bright or the Vian children or someone else who helped with that composite but I know which sketch you mean. <BR> <BR>Thanks for the information about the EAR composites. I hope the guy is still alive &#40;incarcerated&#41; and brought in, for many reasons. He breaks so many traditional profile beliefs that a paper should be written about it. Glad to see you have some skepticism, as do other posters, about profiling on the EAR/ONS boards. I like behavioral profiling to a degree but psychological profiling does more harm than good. &#40;The shrinks overanalyze <i>everything</i>.&#41; Or maybe deep understanding of Freudian bric-a-brac is crucial to capturing ONS or Zodiac; which parent was passive? Figure that out and you know what &#34;type&#34; to look for. Gag me with a cigar! Thanks again, hope you catch that one, I cannot think of a more dangerous serial killer, including BTK.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 9827-->
<!--Post: 9858--><!--Time: 1109663659-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST9858"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bmodule_244@hotmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Bmodule<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bmodule<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, February 28, 2005 - 11:54 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I know that with internet security being what it is today something of this sort would probably be a disaster, but if it could be secured a main posting board similar to this would solve a lot of the &#34;sharing problem&#34; between agencies. <BR> <BR>I&#39;m thinking that all agencies, including police, linked on private intranet with central database &#40;maybe someplace just north of Kechi would be a good spot for the main brain trust&#41;. <BR> <BR>Every criminal case in the U.S. would be issued an access code imprinted on a plastic card. To work a case using the kechi brain trust you would have to be in physical possession of the card and insert it in the computer. each case would have an independent keystroke log and log to record records accessed. At kechi would be databases for specific purposes, messengers for talk, email for email, specific forms to use to access info that would automatically enter search data to databases, all of this being monitored by security techs and investigative techs using peoplesoft-like to monitor and study the flow of successful and unsuccessful investigations to develop better investigative techniques.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 9858-->
<!--Post: 9881--><!--Time: 1109686567-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST9881"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Zenith<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Zenith<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 01, 2005 - 6:16 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I also wholeheartedly agree that BTK didn&#39;t stop killing and I&#39;ve believed all along that we&#39;ll find more and more of his victims. I also never felt that he was trying to get caught. I know there&#39;s the Freudian theory about criminals wanting to get caught, but I think he was just deriving more pleasure from messing with WPD and KAKE. <BR> <BR>I am intrigued by why LE kept so much information secret through the years and hope in the upcoming trial much of it will become public so we can perhaps understand their logic. However, without knowing more details, I cannot help but believe the case would have been solved earlier if more details would have been released to the public.  <BR> <BR>I may be completely wrong. But it seems to me, especially now that we know he was right under everyone&#39;s nose all along, that LE was concerned about copycats and concerned about keeping the public calm. Unfortunately, a 30 year investigation and killing spree cannot be called a success. I believe it was an error not to release more information. I believe more people are dead because of this error. But again, I may be wrong.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 9881-->
</table></td></tr></table>
